[SourceNote "Lightweight file: user rarely sees this opening. Naroditsky teaches the White-side tactical ideas; this Black repertoire uses that transcript mainly as a warning about f7 and loose tactical play."]

1. e4 e5
2. d4 exd4
{Accept the Center Game. White removes Black's e-pawn, but the queen recapture will cost time.}
3. Qxd4 Nc6
{The basic punishment: develop with tempo and make the queen choose a square.}
4. Qd1
{This is the exact weak-deck branch. White has retreated all the way home, so Black should not look for something heroic; just develop quickly and make White prove compensation for the missing d-pawn.}
4... Nf6
{The study move from the gap. It hits e4 and develops. Naroditsky's useful warning from related Center/Danish positions is that White often answers loose pressure on e4 with activity, especially Bc4 pressure on f7, so keep the knight and f7 tactics under control.}
5. Nc3
{White's most natural way to defend e4.}
(5. Bc4
{Naroditsky's key attacking idea in this family: develop with tempo on f7 rather than only reacting to the e4 attack. Do not grab pawns blindly if Bxf7+ or queen checks appear.}
5... Bb4+
{A practical answer: give check, develop, and force White to spend a tempo before the f7 pressure becomes dangerous.}
6. c3 Be7
7. Nf3 d6
8. O-O O-O
{Black has neutralized the immediate f7 story and can continue with ...Re8, ...Bg4, or ...Be6 according to White's setup.})
(5. Bg5
{Pinning is another attempt to keep e4 stable.}
5... Be7
{Break the pin calmly.}
6. Nc3 O-O
7. Nf3 d6
{The simple plan is enough: castle, connect pieces, then challenge the center with ...Re8 or ...d5 when prepared.})
(5. f3
{Naroditsky warns that f-pawn defenses can create dark-square and queen-check tactics in related lines. Here Black can immediately strike the center.}
5... d5
6. exd5 Nxd5
7. Bd3 Be6
{Black has active pieces and no reason to fear the rare queen-home Center Game.})
(5. Bd3
{A solid but passive reaction to the attack on e4.}
5... d5
6. exd5 Nxd5
7. Nf3 Be7
{Black has solved development and can castle.})
5... Bb4
{Pin first. If White is defending e4 with pieces, the bishop check/pin makes the coming ...d5 break easier.}
6. Bd3
(6. Nge2
{White may try to avoid a pin on the c3 knight.}
6... d5
7. exd5 Nxd5
8. Bd2 O-O
{Black is fully developed and the Center Game has not produced an initiative.})
6... d5
{The main practical break. Once White spends time defending e4, Black should challenge the center rather than drift.}
7. exd5 Nxd5
8. Nge2 O-O
9. O-O Be6
{Repertoire summary: after Qd1, Black is already comfortable. Use ...Nf6 to ask about e4, meet active Bc4 tries with development and checks, and use ...d5 once White commits to defending the center.} *
